what homology is, and how it relates to sequence similarity (1);
exact alignment by dynamic programming and the substitution matrices that score it (Section 1.3);
the heuristic and indexing methods that make database search fast, the statistics that tell us which hits to trust, and how a hit becomes an annotation (Section 1.4);
comparing sequences without aligning them, by their \(k\)-mers (Section 1.5);
multiple alignments, motifs, profiles and profile hidden Markov models, which capture whole families and find more distant relationships (Section 1.6);
protein domains and the databases that classify them (Section 1.7);
how the same ideas are extended to structured RNAs, where secondary structure is often conserved better than sequence (Section 1.8);
aligning whole genomes, and the conservation scores computed from multiple genome alignments (Section 1.9);
protein language models and how they are starting to be used to detect remote homology (Section 1.10);
how search methods are benchmarked, and what can go wrong (Section 1.11).

sequence databases: the nucleotide archives, RefSeq, UniProt, metagenomic sequences and protein families (Section 2.3);
genome databases and browsers, model organism databases, annotation formats and coordinate conventions, genome builds and liftover, and taxonomy databases (Section 2.4);
experimental and predicted structures (Section 2.5);
function and pathways: GO, KEGG, Reactome, ontologies and knowledge graphs, and how function prediction is evaluated (Section 2.6);
interactions and networks (Section 2.7);
expression and proteomics repositories (Section 2.8);
medical, variant and cancer data, and controlled access to personal data (Section 2.9).

the origin of life: prebiotic chemistry, the RNA world, metabolism-first scenarios and astrobiology (Section 3.2);
the basic concepts: mutation, selection, drift, effective population size, hitchhiking, the neutral theory and the molecular clock (Section 3.3);
detecting selection with dN/dS and related tests, and testing molecular convergence, using the neutral theory as the null model (Section 3.4);
homologues, orthology inference, orthology databases and gene duplication (Section 3.5); other routes to new genes, including de novo gene birth, and pseudogenes and small proteins are covered in Section 6.8;
protein evolution: domains, repeats, disorder, coevolution, alternative splicing, structural constraints and ancestral sequence reconstruction (Section 3.6);
genome evolution: horizontal gene transfer, the power laws of gene family sizes, and gene-family expansion and contraction (Section 3.7).

the history of the field and the vocabulary of trees: rooting, clades, the number of possible trees and gene trees versus species trees (Section 4.2);
the main families of methods: distance methods, parsimony, maximum likelihood and Bayesian inference (Section 4.3), the substitution models they depend on, the choice between nucleotide, codon and protein data, and tests of the molecular clock (Section 4.3.6), and measures of support (Section 4.3.9);
alignment uncertainty, phylogenomics, gene trees versus species trees, consensus trees and concordance factors, reconciliation, phylogenomic function prediction and phylogenetic networks (Section 4.4);
what has been learned about the tree of life, its root and the origin of eukaryotes; how taxonomy relates to phylogeny; and the evolution of humans and the other great apes (Section 4.5).

sequencing technologies, from Sanger to single-molecule long reads, and the theory of shotgun sequencing and coverage (Section 5.2);
genome assembly: overlap graphs, string graphs and de Bruijn graphs, repeats, scaffolding, haplotypes and assembly quality (Section 5.3);
gene prediction in prokaryotes and eukaryotes, and evidence-based annotation (Section 5.4);
metagenomics: amplicon and shotgun sequencing, profiling, assembly and binning (Section 5.5);
read mapping and variant calling: indexes, SAM and VCF records, mapping quality, genotype likelihoods, structural variants, phasing, targeted sequencing, copy number and somatic variants in cancer (Section 5.6);
regulatory and epigenomic sequencing: ChIP-seq, ATAC-seq, bisulfite and long-read methylation calling, and CLIP-seq (Section 5.7);
human genetics: linkage disequilibrium, genome-wide association studies, fine-mapping, polygenic scores, and the search for Mendelian disease variants by exome and genome sequencing (Section 5.8).

background: nucleic acids, the central dogma, cells and model organisms (Section 6.2);
the genome projects, what a gene is, why the human gene count fell and how assemblies differ from annotations, genome size and gene number, and the C-value paradox (Section 6.3);
the organisation of prokaryotic and eukaryotic genomes, genome duplications, organelle genomes and viral genomes (Section 6.4);
gene order, synteny, dot plots and genome rearrangements (Section 6.5);
genomes in populations: bacterial pangenomes, from one reference genome to a pangenome graph, population-genetic explanations of genome complexity, and what ancient genomes have revealed about human evolution (Section 6.6);
repeats, transposable elements, segmental duplications, the mechanisms that create structural variants, and the 3D organisation of the genome (Section 6.7);
pseudogenes, the birth of new genes (including de novo from non-coding DNA), and small proteins missed by classical annotation (Section 6.8);
non-coding RNAs, regulatory elements and the debate about how much of the genome is functional (Section 6.9).

microarrays, their preprocessing and what they taught us (Section 7.2);
RNA-seq: library preparation, experimental design, quality control, spliced alignment, transcript assembly, quantification and normalisation (Section 7.3);
statistics: the general statistical background, differential expression with count models, design matrices and contrasts, multiple testing, dimensionality reduction, clustering and its validation, and classification with many genes and few samples (Section 7.4);
interpretation: what a change in expression means, gene set analysis, co-expression and eQTLs, and the relation between mRNA and protein levels (Section 7.5);
single-cell genomics: technologies, analysis, integration, pseudobulk testing, multimodal and spatial data, foundation models and cell types (Section 7.6);
single-cell sequencing of cancer and tumour evolution (Section 7.7).

mass spectrometry: history, instruments, bottom-up and top-down proteomics, fragmentation, acquisition strategies (DDA, DIA, targeted) quantification, experimental design and quality control (Section 8.2);
algorithms for peptide identification: database search, spectral libraries, de novo sequencing with deep learning, and the prediction of spectra and retention times (Section 8.3);
statistics: the target–decoy approach, Percolator, the protein inference problem, error control at different levels, normalisation, missing values and differential abundance (Section 8.4);
beyond abundance: post-translational modifications, interaction proteomics and hu.MAP, structural MS and spatial proteomics (Section 8.5);
proteomics without MS: antibody-based proteomics and the Human Protein Atlas, affinity proteomics, multiplexed imaging, protein sequencing and orthogonal validation (Section 8.6);
repositories, standards and the Human Proteome Project (Section 8.7).

background: amino acids, thermodynamics and binding, and enzymes (Section 9.2);
the levels of protein structure and the experimental methods: X-ray crystallography, NMR, cryo-EM and integrative modelling (Section 9.3);
protein folding: Anfinsen’s dogma, stability, Levinthal’s paradox and funnels, and misfolding, amyloid and prions (Section 9.4);
structure comparison, what it has revealed about structure evolution, and the structure databases (Section 9.5 and Section 9.6);
CASP, the blind experiment that has measured progress in prediction since 1994, and its accuracy measures (Section 9.7);
structure prediction, from secondary structure (and how it is assigned from coordinates), homology modelling with loops and side chains, fold recognition, fragment assembly and coevolution to AlphaFold2, AlphaFold3 and co-folding models; intrinsically disordered proteins; the limits of current methods and how to check a predicted model (Section 9.8);
protein language models and structure (Section 9.9);
membrane proteins (Section 9.10);
molecular dynamics, Monte Carlo sampling, convergence and computational cost, normal modes, structures versus ensembles, optimisation and energy functions (force fields and statistical potentials), and docking (Section 9.11);
protein design (Section 9.12).

what function is and how it is described: the Gene Ontology, evidence codes, the structure of GO annotations, and EC numbers (Section 10.2);
how function is determined experimentally: forward and reverse genetics, knockout collections, RNAi and CRISPR screens, DepMap, and why many knockouts have no visible phenotype (Section 10.3);
how function is predicted: transfer by homology and its pitfalls, families and domains, genomic context, networks, structure, functional and modification sites, disordered regions, machine learning, and how evidence is combined (Section 10.4);
subcellular localisation, the sorting signals that determine it, and how their predictors are evaluated (Section 10.5);
predicting the effects of missense variants (Section 10.6);
how function prediction is evaluated, and why that is hard (Section 10.7).

direct physical binding between two proteins;
co-complex membership, in which two proteins belong to the same complex without necessarily touching;
genetic interaction, in which mutations in two genes have a combined effect on the phenotype that differs from the expected one;
regulatory influence, in which one gene product changes the expression or activity of another;
functional association, in which two proteins take part in the same process.



experimental methods: yeast two-hybrid, affinity purification–mass spectrometry (AP-MS) and related methods, hu.MAP, errors, biases and coverage, binding affinity, live-cell proximity and validation, genetic interactions, and gene regulatory networks inferred from perturbations (Section 11.2);
networks: interactome maps, network concepts and their critics, functional association networks and Bayesian integration, pathway and network enrichment analysis, network medicine, systems biology and flux balance analysis, and dynamic models of regulatory networks (feedback, switches, oscillators, robustness and parameter estimation) (Section 11.3);
prediction methods: interfaces, genomic context, coevolution, docking, deep learning with AlphaFold, and how predictions are evaluated (Section 11.4).

the basics: kinds of learning, linear models and regularisation, generalisation and overfitting, the pitfalls of biological data, how to evaluate predictors, and reporting standards (Section 12.2);
the history: the perceptron, neural networks and backpropagation, hidden Markov models, support vector machines and random forests (Section 12.3);
deep learning: training deep networks, convolutional, recurrent, transformer and graph networks, self-attention, language models for proteins and DNA, and interpretability (Section 12.4);
generative models: autoregressive models, autoencoders, GANs and diffusion (Section 12.5);
foundation models and how to evaluate them (Section 12.6);
integrating data: multi-omics integration, latent factors and networks, and what integration can and cannot show about causes (Section 12.7);
AI in research practice: what large language models are, what they have achieved in biology, and how to use AI tools responsibly (Section 12.8).

images as data: kinds of images, classical analysis, software, file formats and repositories (Section 13.2);
deep learning for images: the main tasks, CNNs and U-Net, StarDist and Cellpose, label-free prediction, vision transformers, self-supervised learning and foundation models, and the pitfalls (Section 13.3);
image-based profiling: Cell Painting, morphological profiles and the JUMP and RxRx data sets (Section 13.4);
protein localisation from images: the subcellular atlas of the Human Protein Atlas, image-classification challenges, OpenCell and self-supervised models (Section 13.5);
tissues: multiplexed imaging, spatial omics, spatial neighbourhood analysis and computational pathology (Section 13.6);
cell atlases and AI virtual cells: the Human Cell Atlas, HuBMAP, what a cell type is, the virtual-cell efforts of CZI, the Arc Institute and SciLifeLab’s Alpha Cell, and how well current models work (Section 13.7);
mechanistic and structural models of whole cells, and cryo-electron tomography (Section 13.8).

C.2 Keeping up to date
The field changes faster than any textbook. These sources show what is new:

the proceedings of the most recent ISMB and ECCB conferences (published as supplements of Bioinformatics);
the annual Nucleic Acids Research Database and Web Server issues;
seminars and dissertations at DBB and SciLifeLab.
